}
{\plain {\ulnone \ulnone We have already
 noted this periodicity }{\ulnone \ulnone 
\ulnone \ulnone in}{\ul \b  Exercise 3.3}{\ulnone 
 a}{\ulnone s a direct consequence of the
 period in either n or k of the sequences
 \{exp[i(2}{\f1 \ulnone p}{\ulnone /N)nk]\}}{\ulnone 
. It is this property which allows the basic
 index set to be chosen arbitrarily, as 
long as the N unique values are included.
 C}{\ulnone onventionally the set [0,...,N-1]
 is chosen. (A set symmetric about zero 
might seem more natural: but this can only
 be done for N odd.)}{\ulnone  }}
}
