.TXT 4 0 34 0
Cg a61.250000,60.875000,698
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {As a consequence of Property 4.2,
 it is only necessary to compute the DFT
 for k=0,...,floor(N/2) and then get the
 remaining values by a simple conjugation
 operation. (The Mathcad built-in function
 }{\b floor(x)}{ is the greatest integer
 <= x.) Fast DFT algorithms can take advantage
 of this. For our purposes, though, such
 forms can introduce an impediment to understanding
 the basic ideas of the DFT- which already
 contain sufficient complications!- and 
we will not use them. }{\b0 \fs20 Mathcad
 has, }{\b0 \fs20 \fs20 for real sequences}{\b0 
\fs20 , special fast algorithms for the 
DFT and its inverse, the built-in functions
 }{\b \fs20 fft}{\fs20 \b0  and}{\b \fs20 
 ifft}{\fs20 \b0 . }}
}
.TXT 18 0 35 0
Cg a61.625000,61.250000,411
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b \ulnone Property 4.3: Periodic
 time translation}{  Given a sequence \{f}{\dn8 
k}{, n = 0,...,N-1\}, with  DFT   \{F}{\dn8 
k}{\}, we extend it to have period N. Then,
 for any integer nd, the translated     
  sequence  \{g}{\dn8 n}{=f}{\dn8 n-nd}{\}
 is defined over indices n in \{0,...,N-1\}
 and we may compute       its DFT \{G}{\dn8 
k}{\}. We find that G}{\dn8 k}{ = conj(ec}{\dn8 
nd,k}{)}{ F}{\dn8 k}{ .}}
}
.TXT 12 0 36 0
Cg a42.375000,53.875000,133
