{\plain {\b \ulnone Property 4.5: Plancherel's
 Identity}{  Given a sequence  }{\{f}{\dn8 
n}{, n=0,...,N-1\} }{with DFT \{F}{\dn8 
k}{, k=0,...,N-1\},}}
}
.EQN 8 10 51 0
{0:n}NAME$((|(({0:f}NAME)[({0:n}NAME))))^(2):(1)/({0:N}NAME)*{0:k}NAME$((|(({0:F}NAME)[({0:k}NAME))))^(2)
.TXT 6 -10 52 0
Cg a59.875000,59.875000,631
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We have defined the left side of
 this last relation as the squared norm 
of the sequence \{f}{\dn8 n}{\} regarded
 as a vector in an N-dimensional linear 
vector space. The sum on the right side 
is the squared norm of the DFT sequence 
\{F}{\fs16 \dn6 k}{\}. These squared norms
 are therefore always related by a constant,
 fixed by N. If we had used the orthonormal
 sequences \{ec}{\fs16 \dn6 n,k }{/sqrt(N)\}
 instead of the merely orthogonal \{ec}{\fs16 
\dn6 n,k}{\} in constructing the DFT and
 IDFT, then the 1/N would not appear in 
the statement of Plancherel's Identity: 
the DFT would then be a }{\b unitary}{ transformation.}}
}
