.TXT 33 -2 84 0
Cg a61.250000,61.000000,1009
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {As our notation already signals,}{\ulnone 
 the important application of this result
 to DSP is that we now have an alternative
 method of computing the output of an LIV
 filtering operation defined as the periodic
 convolution}{\ulnone .}{ The procedure 
is as follows:\par \par       }{\b 1}{  
Given a FIR filter length of M1 and a data
 sequence length of M2, choose a \par   
           DFT of size N. For example, if
 we want the periodic and aperiodic     
                       convolutions to agree
 we choose N at least as large as M1+M2-1.\par 
\par       }{\b 2}{  Compute the DFT of 
the FIR filter's impulse response. (This
 need only be                     done once,
 however many input sequences are processed.)\par 
\par      }{\b  3 }{ Compute the DFT of 
the input signal.\par \par       }{\b 4}{
  Multiply the two DFTs.\par \par      }{\b 
 5}{  Compute the IDFT of the product.\par 
\par When the DFT and IDFT algorithms are
 implemented by FFT and IFFT algorithms 
this procedure can be much faster than the
 direct evaluation }{\ul \b (2.5)}{. }}
}
.ATT
.STATUS Go to Section 2, p. 6
.GOTO 2_bk.mcd
.ATT_END
