{\plain {\fs24 \b Fast Fourier Transform}}
}
.TXT 3 10 177 0
Cg a61.125000,62.125000,276
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We indicate how one may speed up
 the computation of the DFT. We assume that
 N is divisible by 2. Then we can rearrange
 the DFT computation so that only about 
one half the required complex operations
 are required, where a complex operation
 is a complex multiply and add. }}
}
.TXT 9 0 178 0
Cg a58.750000,64.000000,81
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We carry along an example with 
which we can time the computation of the
 forms.}}
}
.EQN 5 0 179 0
{0:N}NAME:256
.EQN 0 8 180 0
{0:n}NAME:0;{0:N}NAME-1
.EQN 0 12 181 0
{0:k}NAME:0;{0:N}NAME-1
.EQN 0 12 182 0
({0:f}NAME)[({0:n}NAME):{0:rnd}NAME(2)-1
.EQN 0 13 183 0
{0:W}NAME({0:N}NAME):{0:exp}NAME(-1i*(2*{0:\p}NAME)/({0:N}NAME))
.TXT 5 -45 184 0
Cg a19.250000,64.000000,31
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {The definition of the DFT is}}
}
.EQN 6 6 185 0
{0:W1}NAME:{0:W}NAME({0:N}NAME)
.EQN 0 15 186 0
({0:F.def}NAME)[({0:k}NAME):((0,{0:N}NAME-1,{0:n}NAME,({0:W1}NAME)^({0:n}NAME*{0:k}NAME)*({0:f}NAME)[({0:n}NAME)){64})
.TXT 12 -21 187 0
Cg a61.125000,62.125000,247
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Assuming that the complex exponentials
 are available in negligible time, t}{he
 number of complex operations required for
 this calculation is N}{\fs16 \up6 2}{: 
for each k there are N complex multiply 
adds required and there are N values of 
k. }}
}
.TXT 8 0 188 0
Cg a61.000000,62.000000,162
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Now suppose we simply break the
 above sum into two sums, one over the even
 indices and the other over the odd indices.
 Each such sum will have N2 = N/2 terms.}}
}
.EQN 8 2 189 0
{0:N2}NAME:({0:N}NAME)/(2)
.EQN 0 10 190 0
({0:F.2}NAME)[({0:k}NAME):((0,{0:N2}NAME-1,{0:n}NAME,({0:W}NAME({0:N}NAME))^(2*{0:n2}NAME*{0:k}NAME)*({0:f}NAME)[(2*{0:n2}NAME)){64})+((0,{0:N2}NAME-1,{0:n}NAME,({0:W}NAME({0:N}NAME))^((2*{0:n2}NAME+1)*{0:k}NAME)*({0:f}NAME)[(2*{0:n2}NAME+1)){64})
.TXT 7 -12 191 0
Cg a61.375000,63.000000,174
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Notice that a factor W(N)}{\fs16 
\up6 k}{ can be brought outside the second
 summation. Also, note that W(N)}{\fs16 
\up6 2}{ is W(N/2). So we may write the 
last equation as }}
}
.EQN 8 2 192 0
{0:W2}NAME:({0:W1}NAME)^(2)
.EQN 0 12 193 0
({0:F.2}NAME)[({0:k}NAME):((0,{0:N2}NAME-1,{0:n}NAME,({0:W2}NAME)^({0:n}NAME*{0:k}NAME)*({0:f}NAME)[(2*{0:n}NAME)){64})+({0:W1}NAME)^({0:k}NAME)*((0,{0:N2}NAME-1,{0:n}NAME,({0:W2}NAME)^({0:n}NAME*{0:k}NAME)*({0:f}NAME)[(2*{0:n}NAME+1)){64})
.TXT 7 -14 200 0
Cg a61.875000,62.000000,344
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {The number of complex operations
 required for each sum is now (N/2)}{\fs16 
\up6 2}{. Also, N more are required to multiply
 by W1}{\fs16 \up6 k}{ and add. Thus N}{\fs16 
\up6 2}{/2+N complex operations are required.
 For N large, the number of complex operations
 required to compute the DFT has been approximately
 halved by this simple device. }}
}
.TXT 10 0 194 0
Cg a61.250000,62.000000,794
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b Exercise 4.6}{  You can choose
 N so that the computation times of F}{\fs16 
\dn6 def}{ and F}{\fs16 \dn6 2}{ may be 
easily compared. The value will depend upon
 your computer speed. Make sure that this
 section has been entirely computed. (E.g.,
 select}{\b  Calculate Document}{ from the
 }{\b Math}{ menu.) Then select the F}{\fs16 
\dn6 def}{ equation and press F9, timing
 the computation. Similarly, select the 
F}{\fs16 \dn6 2}{ equation, press F9 and
 time the computation. E.g., for a DX486,
 66 mHz cpu, the two computations times 
were, for N=256, 20 and 11 sec. Note that
 computations of the complex exponentials
 were included in the times: the number 
of such is nearly the same in both expressions,
 N}{\fs16 \up6 2}{ for F}{\fs16 \dn6 def}{
 and N}{\fs16 \up6 2}{+N for F}{\fs16 \dn6 
2}{.}}
}
.TXT 24 0 195 0
Cg a62.375000,62.250000,516
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {If N/2 is also divisible by 2 then
 this process can be repeated. In fact, 
if N is a power of 2, say N = M}{\fs16 \up6 
2}{, then the process can be iterated M 
= log}{\fs16 \dn6 2}{(M) times. It can be
 shown that about 2N log}{\fs16 \dn6 2}{(N)
 complex operations will be required. If
 one makes certain simple modifications 
of the algorithm, namely (i) not multiplying
 by 1 and (ii) factoring out common multiplies
 by W(N), then about }{\b (N/2) log}{\fs16 
\dn6 \b 2}{\b (N)}{ complex operations will
 be required. }}
}
.TXT 16 0 196 0
Cg a57.750000,63.000000,127
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b Exercise 4.7}{  Make a comparison
 of N}{\fs16 \up6 2}{ and (N/2) log}{\fs16 
\dn6 2}{(N) for N=2}{\fs16 \up6 M}{, M=1,2,...12}}
}
.TXT 4 0 197 0
Cg a60.875000,62.000000,451
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {It can be shown that, if N is "highly
 composite", that is, composed of the product
 of many integers, then considerable savings
 in computation may be made. Many mathematical
 applications, including Mathcad, have algorithms
 that can reduce the DFT computation for
 such N. It is good practice to choose such
 N when possible. In this book almost always
 N is chosen as a power of 2. Such an algorithm
 is called a }{\b fast Fourier transform,
 FFT}{.}}
}
.TXT 14 0 198 0
Cg a59.500000,61.750000,154
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
